  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through Monday)\nIssued at 1034 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe main PV max over NM, embedded within the approaching longwave \nupper trough, is expected to dive down into TX and then progress \neastward along the Gulf Coast. Thus, the lift and low to mid level \nmoisture needed for precipitation will stay south of the Red River \nwith this system.\n\nAnother PV max diving south out of Canada on the northern limb of \nthe upper trough is forecast to dive down into the MO Ozarks by \nThanksgiving. There is still considerable disagreement in the \nevolution of the pattern. However, both the GFS and EC wrap moisture \nup and around the northern system, some of which is snow, they just \ndisagree with how far west this occurs. Regardless, it is looking \nhighly unlikely that any precip will wrap far enough west around \nthis system to impact far NE OK and the Ozarks of NW AR. What is \nmost likely is an increase in N to NW winds and some cloud cover on \nthe back side of the developing low pressure system and continued \nbelow average cool temps. Thus, low impact weather is expected for \nthe Thanksgiving day holiday.\n\nOnce the upper low moves east away from the region, a warming trend \nwill ensue beginning Friday on into the first part of the holiday \nweekend. A NW flow pattern aloft looks to evolve over the central \npart of the country during the holiday weekend, with hints of a \nsystem and associated cold front in the multi-model ensemble means \nand deterministic EC possibly impacting the area as soon as Sunday.\n\nLacy",
